        "markdown":
"""# Similarity Search

Similarity search is a vector search that finds items by meaning rather than exact wording. Content and queries are converted into numeric vectors (embeddings), and results are ranked by how close their vectors sit to the query's vector. There are four stages: vectorizing the content, vectorizing the query, running a kNN search, and scoring the results.

**1. Vectorize the content** — an embedding model reads each chunk of text and outputs a fixed-length list of numbers (typically 384–3,072 dimensions). Semantically similar text lands close together in this space. Vectors are normalized to unit length and stored in an index.

**2. Vectorize the query** — at search time, the query goes through the same model. Each model defines its own vector space, so query and content must always use the same model.

**3. Execute the kNN search** — the index finds the k stored vectors closest to the query. Exact kNN is accurate but scans everything; approximate methods (HNSW, IVF, product quantization) trade a small amount of recall for large speed gains on large collections. Metadata filters can narrow the candidate set before or after the vector scan.

**4. Score the results** — each candidate is ranked by cosine similarity, dot product, or Euclidean distance (cosine is most common for text). Raw scores are relative — threshold tuning on real queries decides what counts as a match. Hybrid search blends in keyword scores (BM25) for exact terms; re-ranking passes the top candidates through a cross-encoder for higher accuracy at higher cost.

For a deeper treatment of each stage see [VectorSearch.md](https://github.com/foam-foundation/foam3/blob/development/doc/guides/VectorSearch.md).

### In a Nutshell - From Content to Scored Results

Content is chunked, embedded, and indexed ahead of time. At query time the query is embedded with the same model, the index returns the nearest vectors, and the candidates are scored and optionally re-ranked before being returned to the user."""
